              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em De Donder-Weyl-Hamilton equation
            is a refinement of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equation"} Hamilton's equation
            from a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+derivative"} variational
            direction to many.
          %p
            Where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equation"} Hamilton's equation
            appears as a natural condition in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+geometry"} n-plectic geometry
          %p
            Where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equation"} Hamilton's equation
            appears as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanical+system"} mechanical system
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamics"} dynamics
            is described by evolution along a single parameter (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"}> time
            ), so the
            %em De Donder-Weyl Hamilton equation
            appears as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of a higher dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
            where “evolution” is along more parameters (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"}> spacetime
            ).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            to be regarded as
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \to \Sigma
            be a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
            to be thought of as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
          %p
            For simplicity of exposition we first consider local patches and take without essential restriction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            to be a trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times \Sigma
            %span.newWikiWord
              summation convention
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/summation+convention"} ?
            for summation over repeated induces is understood.
          %p
            We then denote the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi σ
                    %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\sigma^\mu\}
            and those on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\phi^i\}
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (J^1 E^)^\ast
            for the dualized first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\sigma^\mu, \phi^i, \pi_i^\mu\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mi i
                    %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi^\mu_i
            the “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            \-th
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            of the
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %p
            Consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            which is
            = succeed "," do
              %em of first order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mn 1
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^1 E
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
          #ELEquations.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations"} Euler-Lagrange equations
              are
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi μ
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi μ
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial_\mu
                    \\frac{\delta L }{\delta (\partial_\mu \phi^i)}
                    \=
                    \\frac{\delta L}{ \delta \phi^i}
                    \\,.
          #dWHamiltonianByLegendre.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em De Donder-Weyl-Hamiltonian
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is its generalized
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Legendre+transform"} Legendre transform
              (if it exists), the function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ≔
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi μ
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H \coloneqq \pi^\mu_i \partial_\mu \phi^i - L
            %p on the dual jet bundle.
          #DWHEquations.num_prop
            %h6#propositiondefinition Proposition/Definition
            %p
              In terms of the De Donder-Weyl Hamiltonian
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#dWHamiltonianByLegendre"}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equations+of+motion"} Euler-Lagrange equations of motion
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ELEquations"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equations"} differential equations
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi μ
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi μ
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi μ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\partial_\mu \phi^i
                    \=
                    \\frac{\partial H}{\partial \pi_i^\mu}
                    \\;\;\,,
                    \\;\;\;\;
                    \\partial_\mu \pi^\mu_i
                    \=
                    \-
                    \\frac{\partial H}{ \partial \phi^i}
                    \\,.
            %p
              These are called the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong De Donder-Weyl-Hamilton equations
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#AsnplecticHamiltonEquation
            As a multisymplectic/
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-plectic Hamiltonian equation
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in C^\infty(X)
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            regarded as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equations"} Hamilton's equations
            are equivalent to
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mi v
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\iota_v \omega = \mathbf{d}H
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector"} tangent vector
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            = succeed "\u201D-parameter" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
          %p
            By passing from plain phase space to the corresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} dual jet bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            coordinate
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mi v
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\iota_v \Omega = 0
          %p where now
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi ω
                  %mo +
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∧
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi t
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega = \omega + \mathbf{d}H \wedge \mathbf{d}t
                  \\,.
          %p
            This may accordingly be thought of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativity"} relativistic
            version of Hamilton’s equations.
          %p
            We now discuss the analog of both the “non-relativistic” and of this “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativity"} relativistic
            version” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equation"} Hamilton's equation
            for the de Donder-Weyl-Hamilton equation.
          %p
            In both cases, the
            = succeed "-tuple" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vectors"} tangent vectors
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            which satisfies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            is characterized as a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+n-vector+field"} Hamiltonian n-vector field
          %h4#relativistic_form Relativistic form
          #PrePlecticForm.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Given a De Donder-Weyl-Hamiltonian
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#dWHamiltonianByLegendre"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %mi E
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Omega \in \Omega^{n+1}_{cl}((J^1 E)^\ast)
            %p
              on the dual jet bundle (the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+form"} multisymplectic form
              or
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+form"} pre-(n+1)-plectic form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi μ
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mi vol
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi vol
                      %mi Σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Omega
                    \\;\coloneqq \;
                    \\mathbf{d} \phi^i \wedge \mathbf{d} \pi^\mu_i
                    \\wedge (\iota_{\partial_\mu} vol_\Sigma)
                    +
                    \\mathbf{d} H \wedge vol_\Sigma
                    \\,.
          #DWHInRelativisticPlecticForm.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi^i, \pi^\mu_i) \colon \Sigma \to J^1 E^\ast
              of the dualized first
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
              satisfies the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DWHEquations"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vectors"} tangent vectors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi μ
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi μ
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi ν
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi p
                          %mi i
                          %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    v_\mu
                    \\coloneqq \partial_{\mu}
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\mu} \partial_{\phi^i}
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial \pi_i^\nu}{\partial \sigma^\mu} \partial_{p^\nu_i}
            %p
              jointly annihilate the pre-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
              \-plectic form of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PrePlecticForm"}
              in that the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation"} equation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                    %mi ⋯
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\iota_{v_1} \cdots \iota_{v_n}) \Omega
                    \= 0
            %p holds.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              First, the component of this equation which does not contain any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\mu
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi μ
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi H
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{v_i}
                    \\;
                    \\mathbf{d}\phi^i \wedge \mathbf{d}\pi^i_\mu
                    \=
                    \\mathbf{d}H
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is already equivalent to the DWH equation, prop.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DWHEquations"}
            %p
              Second, this already implies that the components of the equation that are proportional to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\mu
              are automatically satisfied; because these components are
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\mu}
                    \\frac{\partial \pi_i^\mu}{\partial \sigma^\nu}
                    \\mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    \-
                    \\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\nu}
                    \\frac{\partial \pi_i^\mu}{\partial \sigma^\mu}
                    \\mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    \=
                    \\frac{\partial H}{\partial \phi^i}\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\nu} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial H}{\partial \pi_i^\mu}\frac{\partial \pi_i^\mu}{\partial \sigma^\nu} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
            %p and inserting the DWH equations on the left makes the left side identically equal to the right hand side.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Proposition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DWHInRelativisticPlecticForm"}
              is indeed true in the general case where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              may be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
              dependent (depend nontrivially on the
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi σ
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma^\mu
          %h4#nonrelativistic_form Non-relativistic form
          %p
            To obtain the “non-relativistic” form of the
            = succeed "-plectic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %em affine
            dual first jet bundle with canonical coordinates
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\sigma^a , \phi^i, \pi^a_i, e \}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+form"} pre-(n+1)-plectic form
              on the affine dual first jet bundle is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ≔
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi a
                      %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi σ
                              %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mi vol
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo +
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi vol
                      %mi Σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\omega
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\mathbf{d}\phi^i \wedge \mathbf{d}\phi^\mu_a
                    \\iota_{\partial_{\sigma^\mu}} vol_\Sigma
                    +
                    \\mathbf{d}e \wedge vol_\Sigma
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Vector fields tangent to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
              of the affine dual first jet bundle are of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi μ
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi μ
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi ν
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi p
                          %mi i
                          %mi ν
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi e
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    v_\mu
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\partial_{\mu}
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\mu} \partial_{\phi^i}
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial \pi_i^\nu}{\partial \sigma^\mu} \partial_{p^\nu_i}
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial e}{\partial \sigma^\mu} \partial_e
                    \\,.
          #DWHInNonRelativisticPlecticForm.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              On the affine dual first jet bundle the de Donder-Weyl-Hamilton equation characterizes those
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vectors"} tangent vectors
              as above satisfy
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mn 1
                    %mi ⋯
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo +
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\iota_{v_1} \cdots \iota_{v_n})
                    \\omega
                    \=
                    \\mathbf{d}(H + e)
          %p
            This has been pointed out in (
            %a{:href => "#Helein02"}> Hélein 02, around equation (4)
            ).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This is a slight variant of the proof of prop.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DWHInRelativisticPlecticForm"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi v
                          %mi i
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi μ
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi H
                    %mo +
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi e
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota_{v_i}
                    \\;
                    \\mathbf{d}\pi^i_\mu \wedge \mathbf{d}\phi^i
                    +
                    \\mathbf{d}e
                    \=
                    \\mathbf{d}H
                    +
                    \\mathbf{d}e
                    \\,.
            %p
              Here the term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}e
              cancels on both sides and leaves the equation equivalent to the DWH equation as in the first step of the proof of prop.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DWHInRelativisticPlecticForm"}
            %p
              Second, the component of the claimed equation proportional to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\mu
              is now
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi μ
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\mu}
                    \\frac{\partial \pi_i^\mu}{\partial \sigma^\nu}
                    \\mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    \-
                    \\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\nu}
                    \\frac{\partial \pi_i^\mu}{\partial \sigma^\mu}
                    \\mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial e}{\partial \sigma^\nu} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    \= 0
                    \\,.
            %p Inserting into this the DWH equation makes it equivalent to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi σ
                          %mi ν
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{\partial H}{\partial \phi^i}\frac{\partial \phi^i}{\partial \sigma^\nu} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial H}{\partial \pi_i^\mu}\frac{\partial \pi_i^\mu}{\partial \sigma^\nu} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    +
                    \\frac{\partial e}{\partial \sigma^\nu} \mathbf{d}\sigma^\nu
                    \= 0
            %p This has a unique solution, up to a global constant, given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi i
                      %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi σ
                      %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    e(\{ \sigma^\nu \})
                    \=
                    \- H(
                    \\{
                    \\phi^i(\{ \sigma^\nu \}),
                    \\pi^\mu_i (\{ \sigma^\nu \})
                    \\}
                    )
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              By the proof of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DWHInNonRelativisticPlecticForm"}
              we have
              %em on shell
              that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo +
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H + e = 0
              and that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mo =
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega = \Omega
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+n-vector+field"} Hamiltonian n-vector field
          %h2#references References
          %p Maybe the first example of what is now called De Donder-Weyl theory appeared in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Constantin+Carath%C3%A9odory"}> Constantin Carathéodory
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Über die Extremalen und geodätischen Felder in der Variationsrechnung der mehrfachen Integrale
          %p Then Weyl and de Donder independently published
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermann+Weyl"}> Hermann Weyl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geodesic fields in the calculus of variations
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Théophile De Donder
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Th%C3%A9ophile+De+Donder"} ?
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Théorie invariante du calcul des variations
          %p Reviews:
          %ul#Helein02
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Donder%E2%80%93Weyl_theory"} De Donder-Weyl theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Narciso+Rom%C3%A1n-Roy"}> Narciso Román-Roy
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Multisymplectic Lagrangian and Hamiltonian Formalisms of Classical Field Theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/SIGMA/2009/100/"} journal
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0506022"} arXiv:math-ph/0506022
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+H%C3%A9lein"}> Frédéric Hélein
                ,
                %em Hamiltonian formalisms for
              %p
                multidimensional calculus of variations and perturbation theory_, (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0212036"}> arXiv:math-ph/0212036
                )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+V.+Kanatchikov"} Igor V. Kanatchikov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric (pre)quantization in the polysymplectic approach to field theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Differential Geometry and Its Applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0112263"} arXiv:hep-th/0112263
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://conferences.math.slu.cz/8icdga/PDF/309-322.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+V.+Kanatchikov"}> Igor V. Kanatchikov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em De Donder-Weyl theory and a hypercomplex extension of quantum mechanics to field theory
                %strong 43
                (1999) 157-170 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9810165"}> arXiv:hep-th/9810165
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0034-4877(99)80024-X"} doi:10.1016/S0034-4877(99)80024-X
          %p
            For
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/teleparallel+gravity"} teleparallel gravity
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+V.+Kanatchikov"}> Igor V. Kanatchikov
              \.
              %em The De Donder-Weyl Hamiltonian formulation of TEGR and its quantization
              (2023) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10052"}> arXiv:2308.10052
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            For more see the references at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+geometry"} n-plectic geometry
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+n-vector+field"} Hamiltonian n-vector field
